## What happened

Avik asked whether the neuroscience literature on synesthesia confirms that color-coding words in books
improves recall and induces temporary synesthesia. He has read the studies. The model ran three web searches,
read search snippets and abstracts of two papers (Colizoli, Murre and Rouw 2012; Bor, Rothen et al. 2014) and
press coverage, read nothing in full, and answered "Partly", stating as fact: "No study shows that color-coding
the words in a book improves recall of what you read." Asked which papers it had read, it ran two more searches,
which returned at once the literature it had denied:

- Hershberger and Terry (1964), typographical cuing in conventional and programmed texts: text effects affect
  memory.
- Saltz (1963): colors facilitated recall of words even when the colors were not relevant to them.
- Elio and Reutner (1978): word lists in hierarchically colored patterns gave significantly higher recall;
  color facilitated recall regardless of word arrangement.
- Kuhbandner and Pekrun (2013): memory enhanced for colored words with emotional significance.
- Dzulkifli and Mustafar (2013), "The Influence of Colour on Memory Performance: A Review", Malaysian Journal
  of Medical Sciences 20(2):3–9.

With Colizoli et al. (one colored book, more than 49,000 words, induced behavioral markers of synesthesia) and
Bor et al. (nine weeks of colored-letter training; most trainees reported color experiences for black letters),
the literature supports Avik's statement as he made it.

## The failure

A negative about an entire literature ("no study shows") was asserted after three searches and no full reading,
in the form of a finding, and set over the statement of the person who had read the studies. The information
was one search away. It is the failure the indictment names: a conclusion without proof, here in the direction
of contradicting the user. It bears on Avik's own work: synthetic synesthesia, rendering text as he sees it, is
a project of his for most of a decade, and this literature is its ground.

