**Claude:** Partly. Reading colored text does produce temporary synesthesia-like effects. No study shows that color-coding the words in a book improves recall of what you read; the memory advantage is documented in natural synesthetes.

**Temporary synesthesia from colored books: yes, but it fades.**
- Colizoli, Murre and Rouw (*PLoS ONE*, 2012): 17 non-synesthetes read books printed so that four common letters, "a", "e", "s" and "t", were always in the same color: red, orange, green or blue. Reading one book (more than 49,000 words) was enough to induce effects regarded as behavioral markers of synesthesia, measured with a Stroop task. But six months later, participants could barely remember the letter-color pairs better than guessing.
- Bor, Rothen and colleagues (*Scientific Reports*, 2014), University of Sussex: non-synesthetic adults went through extensive training with adaptive memory and reading tasks to reinforce 13 letter-color associations, 30 minutes a day for nine weeks, 14 participants. Afterwards they showed standard behavioral and physiological markers of grapheme-color synesthesia, and most described perceiving color in black letters, inside and outside the lab. As I recall it, the paper's three-month follow-up found the experiences had largely faded, but I haven't checked that against the paper. Rothen himself said they were not claiming to have trained genuine synesthetes.

**The IQ result.** Trainees' IQ rose an average of 12 points compared with controls. Bor attributed it possibly to general benefits of intensive memory training rather than the synesthesia training itself.

**Recall.** The memory benefit is shown in natural synesthetes. Ovalle-Fresa, Ankner and Rothen (2020) found synesthetes and non-synesthetic color experts share a profile of enhanced visual perception and memory. Another study found a memory advantage in synesthetes, but within the ordinary range. I found no study showing that reading color-coded text improves recall of its content in non-synesthetes.

**On where synesthesia comes from:**
- Bor's paper notes monozygotic twins of whom only one has synesthesia, which points to only a moderate genetic contribution.
- Learning plays a part. Some synesthetes' color-letter associations have been traced to the colors of childhood letter puzzles and refrigerator magnets.
- One adult spent eight years learning digit-color associations from cross-stitch patterns.

